+# phlink update protocol
+
+**Status**: locked for Phase 10 (v1.0 alpha).
+**Spec target**: TUF v1.0.32 subset (delegated targets, hash-agility, and
+multi-repo TAP 4 are out of scope for v1.0).
+**Crypto policy**: Ed25519 only. SHA-256 only. boringssl-backed
+(`crypto::SignatureVerifier`).
+
+This document is the on-the-wire contract between the phlink browser and
+the phlink update CDN. Anything **not** in this document is **not** part
+of the contract — clients MUST reject metadata that contains unknown
+required fields and MUST ignore unknown optional fields.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Repository layout
+
+The update CDN is a static-file HTTPS origin. There is no dynamic
+backend. For v1.0 alpha there is exactly one channel: `stable`.
+
+```
+https://updates.phlink.dev/
+  stable/
+    metadata/
+      1.root.json          # version-prefixed; latest is also at root.json
+      root.json            # always == latest
+      targets.json         # signed by `targets` role
+      snapshot.json        # signed by `snapshot` role
+      timestamp.json       # signed by `timestamp` role
+    targets/
+      phlink-{semver}-mac-arm64.zip
+      phlink-{semver}-mac-x64.zip
+      phlink-{semver}-linux-x64.AppImage
+      phlink-{semver}-linux-x64.deb
+      phlink-{semver}-linux-x64.rpm
+      phlink-{semver}-win-x64.exe
+      phlink-{semver}-win-x64.msi
+```
+
+Clients fetch in this fixed order on every check:
+
+1. `timestamp.json` (small, expires fastest).
+2. If `timestamp.meta["snapshot.json"].version` ≠ the cached snapshot
+   version → fetch `snapshot.json`.
+3. If `snapshot.meta["targets.json"].version` ≠ the cached targets
+   version → fetch `targets.json`.
+4. Look up the target whose name matches the running platform and arch.
+
+This staircase means a no-op check is a single GET of `timestamp.json`.
+
+## 2. Roles
+
+| Role        | Threshold | Expiration | Held by   | Signs                              |
+|-------------|-----------|------------|-----------|------------------------------------|
+| `root`      | 2 of 3    | 365 d      | offline   | top-level role keys (incl. itself) |
+| `targets`   | 1 of 1    | 90 d       | online    | `targets.json`                     |
+| `snapshot`  | 1 of 1    | 30 d       | online    | `snapshot.json`                    |
+| `timestamp` | 1 of 1    | 7 d        | online    | `timestamp.json`                   |
+
+Root keys live on hardware tokens (YubiKey FIPS or equivalent). Targets,
+snapshot, and timestamp keys live in CI secrets and are rotated every 6
+months.
+
+Clients pin the **public** half of the initial root keyset at build time
+via `chrome/browser/phlink/updater/keys/dev_root.json` (selected by build
+flag `phlink_updater_dev_root` for dev builds) or
+`prod_root.json` (release builds). Subsequent root rotations are picked
+up by walking version-numbered `N.root.json` files signed by the previous
+root quorum.
+
+## 3. Canonical JSON
+
+All signed metadata uses TUF's canonical JSON encoding before signature:
+
+- UTF-8.
+- No leading/trailing whitespace.
+- Object keys sorted lexicographically.
+- No insignificant whitespace inside the value.
+- Numbers as the smallest decimal representation, no trailing zeros.
+
+phlink's implementation re-emits parsed values via a small canonicalizer
+in `metadata_client.cc` rather than trusting the server's byte stream;
+this protects against subtle encoder differences between languages.
+
+## 4. Metadata schemas
+
+### 4.1 root.json
+
+```json
+{
+  "signed": {
+    "_type": "root",
+    "spec_version": "1.0.32",
+    "version": 1,
+    "expires": "2027-04-30T00:00:00Z",
+    "consistent_snapshot": false,
+    "keys": {
+      "<key_id>": {
+        "keytype": "ed25519",
+        "scheme": "ed25519",
+        "keyval": { "public": "<hex>" }
+      }
+    },
+    "roles": {
+      "root":      { "keyids": ["..."], "threshold": 2 },
+      "targets":   { "keyids": ["..."], "threshold": 1 },
+      "snapshot":  { "keyids": ["..."], "threshold": 1 },
+      "timestamp": { "keyids": ["..."], "threshold": 1 }
+    }
+  },
+  "signatures": [
+    { "keyid": "<key_id>", "sig": "<hex>" }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+`<key_id>` is the lowercase hex of `SHA-256(canonical_json(public_key))`.
+
+### 4.2 targets.json
+
+```json
+{
+  "signed": {
+    "_type": "targets",
+    "spec_version": "1.0.32",
+    "version": 4,
+    "expires": "2026-07-30T00:00:00Z",
+    "targets": {
+      "phlink-1.0.0-mac-arm64.zip": {
+        "length": 184283921,
+        "hashes": { "sha256": "<hex>" },
+        "custom": { "channel": "stable", "platform": "mac", "arch": "arm64" }
+      }
+    }
+  },
+  "signatures": [...]
+}
+```
+
+`custom` is opt-in; clients MUST tolerate its absence.
+
+### 4.3 snapshot.json
+
+```json
+{
+  "signed": {
+    "_type": "snapshot",
+    "spec_version": "1.0.32",
+    "version": 12,
+    "expires": "2026-05-30T00:00:00Z",
+    "meta": {
+      "targets.json": { "version": 4 }
+    }
+  },
+  "signatures": [...]
+}
+```
+
+Per TUF spec ≥1.0 we only carry `version` (not `length`/`hashes`) for
+non-target metadata. `consistent_snapshot=false` so version-prefixed
+filenames are not used for `targets.json` / `snapshot.json`.
+
+### 4.4 timestamp.json
+
+```json
+{
+  "signed": {
+    "_type": "timestamp",
+    "spec_version": "1.0.32",
+    "version": 312,
+    "expires": "2026-05-07T00:00:00Z",
+    "meta": {
+      "snapshot.json": {
+        "version": 12,
+        "length": 1283,
+        "hashes": { "sha256": "<hex>" }
+      }
+    }
+  },
+  "signatures": [...]
+}
+```
+
+`timestamp.json` carries `length` + `hashes` for `snapshot.json` so a
+client can reject a malicious `snapshot.json` before parsing it.
+
+## 5. Verification rules (client-side)
+
+For every check, the client walks roles in this order:
+
+1. **Root chain**: start from the pinned root version; walk forward
+   through `N+1.root.json`, `N+2.root.json`, … each signed by the
+   previous root quorum (m of n). Stop when the next file 404s. The
+   final root in the chain MUST NOT be expired.
+2. **Timestamp**: signed by ≥ threshold of the current root's `timestamp`
+   key set; not expired; `version` MUST be ≥ cached timestamp version
+   (rollback rejection).
+3. **Snapshot**: byte length and SHA-256 MUST match what `timestamp.json`
+   declared; signed by the current snapshot keyset; not expired;
+   `version` ≥ cached snapshot version.
+4. **Targets**: `version` MUST match what `snapshot.json` declared;
+   signed by the current targets keyset; not expired.
+
+Any failure is fatal for the check. Failures log a structured entry to
+`update.log` and increment an in-memory failure counter; the counter is
+not persisted and never leaves the device.
+
+## 6. Threat model
+
+| Attack                           | Mitigation                                            |
+|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|
+| MITM substitutes `timestamp.json` | Signature check against pinned root's timestamp key  |
+| Rollback to old vulnerable build  | `version` monotonicity on every role                 |
+| Freeze (serve stale metadata)     | `expires` field on every role                        |
+| Mix-and-match across snapshots    | `snapshot.json` pins exact `targets.json` version    |
+| Compromise of online keys         | Root rotation; expiration limits damage window       |
+| Compromise of CDN                 | All trust derives from offline root; CDN can't lie  |
+| Endless data attack on payload    | `length` from `targets.json` capped before download  |
+| Slow loris on download            | Per-byte read deadline in `network::SimpleURLLoader` |
+
+Out of scope: physical access to the user's machine, kernel compromise,
+malicious extensions running in the browser process.
+
+## 7. Key rotation
+
+Quarterly cadence target:
+
+- **Timestamp** key: rotated every 90 days (manual; pre-publish a
+  `timestamp.json` re-signed under the new key alongside one under the
+  old key for a 7-day overlap).
+- **Snapshot** key: rotated every 180 days.
+- **Targets** key: rotated every 365 days.
+- **Root** key: rotated only on compromise or 365-day expiration. Every
+  rotation increments the root version and is signed by both the
+  previous quorum and the new quorum.
+
+The full ceremony is documented in
+`docs/dev/release-keys.md` (deliverable of plan 10-08).
+
+## 8. Privacy
+
+The update check is a single HTTPS GET. The client sends:
+
+- `User-Agent: phlink/{version} ({os}; {arch})`.
+- No cookies. No `Authorization`. No other custom headers. No query
+  parameters.
+- Source IP is observable to the CDN (unavoidable). phlink does not
+  fingerprint or log this on the server side beyond standard CDN access
+  logs (rotated, not joined to any other identity).
+
+There is **no** install ID, machine ID, profile ID, or telemetry beacon.
+There is **no** opt-in path to enable any of the above in v1.0. This is
+covered by PRD §10.1 hard rule and Phase 10 CONTEXT D-09.
+
+## 9. Failure surfacing
+
+Update check / download failures are recorded in:
+
+- `~/Library/Application Support/phlink/update.log` (macOS),
+- `~/.config/phlink/update.log` (Linux),
+- `%LOCALAPPDATA%\phlink\update.log` (Windows),
+
+rotated at 1 MB. The log is plain UTF-8 with one structured record per
+line:
+
+```
+2026-05-04T12:34:56Z level=warn role=timestamp code=expired
+  cached_version=312 server_version=312 expires=2026-04-30T00:00:00Z
+```
+
+The `chrome://settings/help` UI surfaces only the most recent failure
+(human-readable). No remote crash beacon.
+
+## 10. Versioning of this protocol
+
+This document is versioned alongside the phlink binary. The on-the-wire
+TUF protocol does not encode a phlink-protocol-version because the
+underlying TUF `spec_version` field already covers it. Any
+phlink-specific extension (e.g. a future channel switcher) MUST land
+behind a build flag and a dev-channel rollout before being made required.
+
